<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Batawana Chiefdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1795 CE – 1885 CE (approx.) · capital Toteng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Tswana chiefdom of Ngamiland around the Okavango delta, founded by a breakaway branch of the Bangwato.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1795 Tawana, a son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamangwato">Bangwato</a> chief, broke away after a succession dispute and led his followers north-west. They eventually settled near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ngami">Lake Ngami</a> at the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okavango-delta">Okavango</a> delta, where they established authority over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wayeyi">BaYei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hambukushu">Hambukushu</a> and other local peoples.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Batawana chiefs ruled from towns near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ngami">Lake Ngami</a>, drawing tribute in labour and goods from subject peoples. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngamiland">Ngamiland</a> was rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrich-feather">ostrich feathers</a>, and the chiefdom traded with Cape and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> hunters and merchants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a> reached Lake Ngami in 1849 during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/letsholathebe-i">Letsholathebe I</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngamiland">Ngamiland</a> was brought into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bechuanaland-protectorate">Bechuanaland Protectorate</a> in the 1880s and 1890s, and the chiefdom continued under colonial rule. In 1915 the Batawana capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maun">Maun</a>, which grew into the main town of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1795 CE – 1885 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:95.42%;width:1.79%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1795 CE – 1885 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Toteng</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tawana, Letsholathebe I, Moremi II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Tswana religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Setswana</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/botswana">Botswana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tawana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1840&amp;focus=batawana-chiefdom" aria-label="Batawana Chiefdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5298 2932 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Batawana Chiefdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5618 3188L5708 3283M5618 3188L5688 3341"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5708 3283h0M5688 3341h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5618 3188h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5618 3188h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Toteng. <a href="/atlas?year=1840&amp;focus=batawana-chiefdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
